    const string canonical = "https://www.epegboard.com/guides/badminton-doubles-positioning";

    var faqPairs = new (string Q, string A)[]
    {
        ("Should doubles partners play front-and-back or side-by-side?",
         "Both. Front-and-back is the normal attacking shape because one player can pressure from the rear while the other intercepts weak replies. Side-by-side is the normal defensive shape because two players need to cover the width against a smash. In flat exchanges you may be roughly level while both pairs fight for the next attack."),
        ("Does a lift mean we should go side-by-side?",
         "Your lift usually does, because it gives the opponents time to get behind the shuttle and attack downwards. Their lift does the opposite: it normally gives your pair the attack. Read what the next hitter can do rather than reacting to the fact that the shuttle travelled upwards."),
        ("Who takes the middle in doubles defence?",
         "There is no single rule that solves every pair, especially when handedness differs. The important thing at club level is to have an understood convention and to communicate. A simple agreement beats two players each assuming the other owns the middle."),
        ("Should I follow my smash to the net?",
         "Usually not if you smashed from the rearcourt and your partner already owns the front. You normally stay responsible for the rear attack. Move forward when the rally or your own movement has put you into the front role, not merely because you hit a strong shot."),
        ("Does this apply to mixed doubles?",
         "The same ideas of attack, defence and transition still matter, but mixed doubles uses additional positional conventions and role preferences. This guide is a level-doubles baseline rather than a complete mixed-doubles system.")
    };

